---
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";

import StudioAppLayout from "../../layouts/StudioAppLayout.astro";

const piece = await getEntry("projects", "tech-ontology");
if (!piece) throw new Error("Missing content entry: projects/tech-ontology");
---

<StudioAppLayout
  title={piece.data.name}
  description={piece.data.description}
  appSrc="/studio/tech-ontology/app/index.html"
  appTitle="Technology Atlas showing chip families and the processes that make them"
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Studio</p>
    <h1>{piece.data.name}</h1>
    <p>
      Technical knowledge is plentiful but fragmented across papers, patents, product documentation,
      and industry shorthand. What is hard to see is how a principle becomes a process, component,
      system, and product, or where one technology depends on, replaces, or constrains another.
    </p>
    <p>
      Technology Atlas models those relationships directly. It organizes each kind of object in its
      own hierarchy, then connects them with typed links for composition, dependency,
      implementation, succession, and substitution. The result is an explorable map of where a
      technology comes from, what it relies on, and where it may lead.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/studio/">Back to Studio</a>
  </Fragment>
</StudioAppLayout>
